review_text:
enjoy story? like smashing buttons and pretty lights? like seriously how adhd are you? if you answered yes to any of these you should give sword art online fractured daydream a try. description with three epic multiplayer modes its hard to pin down think fortnite versus monsters pitting teams against on another to score higher.

you unlock modes by doing story until you get co op quests.  think of the story as the tutorial it brings you into gameplay mechanics through story. personally i love this part. as you progress through the story you unlock more characters to play. if you really need to you can skip most cutscenes. WHY other than time would you want to?  

honestley really great game not for everyone however its a great melding of game genres. waaay go peoples yall have made a great game. hands down looking forward to raise my ranks. :}

Iam actually want to give a thumbs up but there are still many things need to be fixed.

what I like:
- love the art style, its totally cute!
- character customization, we can change clothes and hair styles. there are many options of them;
- we can have babies (max 2) boy or girl, they can grow up and study on school or help our farm or work for other villager and we can collect their wages;
- our wife / husband can do the same thing as above;
- we can go old and died, and we can play as one of our children;
- many 'difficulty' configuration, so you can play the game as you wish. challenging, cozy or relaxing? its your choice and you can change it anytime during the game!

-What I dislike:
- Craftmanship we build is too cheap! its totally not worth to sell;
- no 'nemesis' system. you can insult and make someone as your enemy but their family / legacy wont hate you (if you dont harass them);
- coffee should revover some stamina (or sleepy bar) rather than speed up the movement;
- a bug when you relocate your house somewhere on the farm, when you go out from your house you will spawn at your house origin location (it happened everytime you load the game);
- no sprinkle system (I know sprinkle invented in the end of 1800s and the game is in 1700s, but since the farm is huge, we need a watering system. especially when the farm helpers are in holiday). *yeah I know I can use rain potion. but, still..
- family (farm helpers) cant refill the watering can (you need steel watering can to make it efficient);
- family (farm helpers) cant take care animals;
- cant put object above another object. I want to make outdoor paths and put some fences on it, but it cant.
- event is too few;
- no characters lore (even the ending wont make you sad or feel simpathy because there is no character / relationship building with the NPCs. the NPCs only sell, buy, or give you quests!)
- no sorting system for inventory.

another info:
this game contains DEI.

Overall not my favourite Yakuza experience, but I still enjoyed it.

Here are my highlights:
- Dragon Spirit;
- The game now includes side stories on top of substories. I liked most of them, and the substories are still pleasant;
- Haruka's story and gameplay will be a hit of miss, but I enjoyed them;
- I did like how all stories tied together;
- I liked how you could choose who to play as during the later chapters;
- The speeches about dreams.

Here are the things I enjoyed a bit less:
- I thought the story was overextending its welcome, especially by the end (although this could also be me blazing through every Yakuza game so fast);
- Shinada. I like the character, but I found that by the time he is introduced, it was hard to yet again "restart" another story (even if I knew it'd
eventually become intertwined with the main story). By that point, you've already played as 4 characters and the story is well-established, with several hooks;
- With so many characters, some gameplay is bound to be less interesting to you, and I found that to be Shinada's case (I liked his fighting style less).

Minor Spoilers!
Pros;
       Nostalgia, Rengeki, Awakening, scenery, great soundtrack, great voice actors(mostly), interesting characters, affection system, customizable weapons, classic jrpg feel. 
Cons;
       Somewhat repetitive, leveling is too easy, level scaling damage makes enemies a few levels lower than you seem pathetically weak, Volume 4 takes about 20 minutes to complete and was a total waste of time, plus the animated cut scenes in volume 4 do not play well, not to mention Haseos 5th form takes over and makes your previous skills and weapons unusable.

With all of this in mind, I would still recommend this game to any anime, or classic rpg fans out there. Its still alot of fun, and the story is wild. Especially recommended to any sword art fans out there since this is a remake of the game/anime that sword art ripped off.
